  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nScattered showers and a few thunderstorms were occurring across\nnorthern WI and Upper Michigan this afternoon. The scattered\nshowers and storms will continue for much of the rest of the\nafternoon, with a gradual southward progression of the rain\nduring the latter half of the afternoon. Based on model \nsoundings, inverted V structure in the low levels of the sounding\nwould suggest gusty winds to 40-45 mph are still possible with\nthe stronger showers and storms along with brief heavy rain. \nAny lingering rain should end early this evening with clearing\nskies during the night with lows mainly in the 50s with 60s along\nthe bay and Lake Michigan.\n\nDry conditions should prevail on Thursday. The only concern is a\nbackdoor front will move across northeast Wisconsin during the\nafternoon where that may be the focus for an isolated shower or \nstorm. Dr conditions prevail on Friday. The forecast becomes\nmuddled late Friday night through the weekend. There appears there\nwill be a few rounds of showers and thunderstorms over the weekend\ninto early next week. This would be good news to help alleviate \nthe drought across our area. Latest model runs suggest the first\nround of showers and would arrive late Friday night into Saturday. \nThere is a lot of questions in where convection would set up as \nthe ECMWF/Canadian model support convection mainly across the \nnorth through Saturday afternoon. The GFS/NAM were further south. \nThe uncertainties then persist through the rest of the weekend \ninto the middle of next week. It is hard to quantify the severe \nweather risk due to the differences in the model output this far \nout. Potential for heavy rain is there with the few rounds of \nstorms. \n\nFor temperatures, a cool night is in store across the north\nThursday morning with lows in the 40s. Otherwise, temperatures\nshould rise to above normal levels over the weekend into the first\nhalf of next week.",
